      "claim": "Ampicillin, a small zwitterionic acid, has 29% oral bioavailability in fasting female subjects, supporting F ≥ 20% for acidic small molecules; the L2 calf value of 12% is species-specific and less transferable.",
      "card_ids": ["C07", "C21"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Perindoprilat, the closest ACE-inhibitor active-acid analog, reached 30 ± 6% systemic availability after oral perindopril in cirrhotic patients, but L2 context indicates normal volunteers had 19 ± 7% after prodrug administration, limiting direct transfer to the query's no-condition setting.",
      "card_ids": ["C08", "C23"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Phenylbutyric acid has 78% absolute oral bioavailability in humans, supporting absorption of small aromatic carboxylic acids.",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C24"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "associated_nonvoting_records",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "contradictory",
      "decision_effect": "weakened",
      "card_ids": ["C19", "C21", "C23", "C24", "C25"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement for zofenoprilat itself is available; the closest ACE-inhibitor active-acid context (perindoprilat) reports metabolite availability after prodrug administration, not direct F of the diacid.",
    "The perindoprilat normal-volunteer value of 19 ± 7% is below the threshold but is an L2 contextual detail and reflects systemic availability after oral perindopril, not direct oral zofenoprilat absorption.",
    "The query's low logD (-1.7) and full ionization at pH 7.4 raise permeability concerns, but no direct permeability, efflux, or first-pass data are supplied.",
    "Species differences limit transfer of the ampicillin calf value (12%) versus the human fasting value (29%).",
    "No data on first-pass metabolism or clearance of zofenoprilat are provided."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F ≥ 20%) because multiple transferable human records for small acidic/peptide-like molecules (pidotimod 42–44%, ampicillin 29%, phenylbutyric acid 78%, perindoprilat 30% in cirrhosis) support the threshold, and no direct query-specific low outcome is available. New L2 evidence introduces caveats (perindoprilat normal 19 ± 7% after prodrug, ampicillin calves 12%) that weaken but do not overturn the supportive direction; confidence remains low."
}

